✅ Call the *verified* Xiamen Public Security Bureau duty desk at **0592-110** (the national emergency number) or their official non-emergency line listed on `fj.xm.police.cn` to report this scam;  
✅ Contact my bank’s official customer service (via the number on my card or their verified app) to alert them about potential fraud linked to my account;  
✅ Take screenshots of the message *without clicking any link*, and save the original SMS metadata (sender number, time stamp);  
✅ *Not* download anything, *not* share documents, *not* enable screen sharing—and certainly *not* call the number provided in the message.

If there were truly an investigation involving me, officers would visit my registered residence or workplace with official identification, present a stamped *Notice of Inquiry* or *Notice of Summoning*, and follow due process—not ambush me with panic-inducing texts.

This isn’t caution—it’s self-protection. And as someone who values both the law *and* my rights, I know: real authorities protect citizens from scams like this… not perpetrate them.

