As Ms. Li Hua—a long-time resident of Guangzhou and a social security beneficiary—I know the Guangzhou Social Security Bureau *never* contacts citizens via unsolicited text messages asking for sensitive information like bank login credentials, PINs, or SMS verification codes. They also *never* ask people to download third-party apps like Zoom (let alone a suspiciously misspelled “Z00m”), add strangers on QQ, or share screens to enter banking details.

This message has multiple red flags:  
🔹 Fake case reference number format (official notices don’t use hyphens like “GZSSB-20240615-0287” in SMS);  
🔹 Urgent 24-hour deadline—classic scam pressure tactic;  
🔹 Request for *bank card username/password* and *transaction PIN*—absolutely prohibited under Chinese financial and data protection laws (e.g., the Personal Information Protection Law);  
🔹 QQ number “3085” is far too short—legitimate government accounts use verified, longer IDs with official blue badges;  
🔹 The “Z00m” spelling is deliberately misleading to bypass filters;  
🔹 Official bureaus *do not conduct identity verification via video call for reimbursement audits*—all verifications are done in person at service halls or through secure, authenticated online platforms (like the Guangdong Government Services Network or the “China Social Security” app).

What I *would* do immediately:  
✅ Delete the message—no reply, no click, no download.  
✅ Call the *real* Guangzhou Social Security Service Hotline at **12333** (the nationally unified, toll-free number)—not the number listed in the fake message—to verify if any audit exists.  
✅ Log in *only* to the official “Guangdong Human Resources and Social Security” mobile app or website (via verified QR code from their official WeChat account @广州人社) to check my reimbursement history and notice status.  
✅ Report the scam to the Guangzhou Cyber Police via the “Net Police” app or by forwarding the SMS to **12321** (China’s national reporting center for spam and fraud).  
✅ Inform my family—especially elderly relatives—to stay vigilant against similar messages.

I’ve been paying into social security for over 20 years. I trust the system—but I also know that real authorities protect citizens’ data, they don’t demand it over QQ. This isn’t compliance—it’s a trap. And I won’t walk into it.
